🚀 Option 1: Docker Compose Secrets (BEST for you)
1. Create secret files

Go to the Inside project folder:
cd fastapi-service

echo "mr111802" > exchange_username.txt
echo "Canada!!!!123" > exchange_password.txt
echo "mr111802@primebank.com.bd" > exchange_email.txt

2. Update docker-compose.yml
services:
  fastapi:
    build: ./fastapi-service
    container_name: fastapi
    ports:
      - "8000:8000"
    networks:
      - proxy

    secrets:
      - exchange_username
      - exchange_password
      - exchange_email

    environment:
      EWS_URL: "https://webmail.primebank.com.bd/EWS/Exchange.asmx"

    command: uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000

networks:
  proxy:

secrets:
  exchange_username:
    file: ./exchange_username.txt
  exchange_password:
    file: ./exchange_password.txt
  exchange_email:
    file: ./exchange_email.txt
🐍 3. Update your FastAPI code

Now read from Docker secrets:

def read_secret(path: str):
    with open(path, "r") as f:
        return f.read().strip()


USERNAME = read_secret("/run/secrets/exchange_username")
PASSWORD = read_secret("/run/secrets/exchange_password")
EMAIL = read_secret("/run/secrets/exchange_email")

EWS_URL = os.getenv("EWS_URL")
🔐 Why this is secure

✔ Stored outside container image
✔ Not in environment variables (safer than -e)
✔ Only mounted at runtime
✔ Works well in production + CI/CD


🔐 What happens when password changes?

With Docker Compose Secrets:

✔ You only update the secret file

Example:

echo "NewPassword123!" > exchange_password.txt
✔ Then restart the container (NO rebuild)
docker compose down
docker compose up -d



if images build into dev pc and transfer to server.
The secret files should exist on the server where Docker Compose runs.

Example on server:

cd /your-project-folder

echo "mr111802" > exchange_username.txt
echo "Canada!!!!123" > exchange_password.txt
echo "mr111802@primebank.com.bd" > exchange_email.txt

Then start container:

docker compose up -d
